WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Look at this diagram. On the left, we have the native heap. On the right, the JavaScript heap.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.500
In a brown field application, these two environments operate in total isolation.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.500
They are like two people working in soundproof rooms, who cannot see each other's whiteboards.

4
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.500
There is no shared global memory.

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:23.000
Every piece of data that moves between them must be explicitly handed through a very narrow slot.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
The absolute simplest way to pass that data is through initial props.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.500
This is channel 1. When the native side creates the react native surface,

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.500
it bundles up the necessary data and slides it through the slot to the JS environment, right as it initializes.

9
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.500
But this delivery mechanism has a strict limitation. It happens exactly once.

10
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:46.000
After the surface is mounted, that initial data becomes immutable.

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.500
You cannot use this channel to send updates later.

12
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:54.000
Because you cannot natively share global memory, you have to abandon the assumption of seamless data access.

13
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Every interaction between your native and JS layers requires an explicit, carefully planned handoff.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
This structural table shows how we handle ongoing operations beyond that initial mount.

15
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.000
If the JS layer needs to request data synchronously or asynchronously, it calls native modules or turbo modules.

16
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.000
That's channel 2. If the native side needs to push system alerts like a network status change,

17
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.000
without waiting for a JS request, it uses event emitters. That is channel 3.

18
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.000
If native code needs to update an already mounted user interface, it uses the surface API, which is channel 4.

19
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Native cannot directly call a react set state.

20
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Instead, the configuration changes flow through the surface API and arrive on the JS side as an ordinary re-render with updated properties.

21
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
So, consider this scenario. Your native layer needs to trigger an action in JS,

22
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
like clearing the text inputs of a react native form.

23
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Which channel do you use? You could use an imperative native to JS call, which is channel 5.

24
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
But the architecturally cleaner pattern involves flipping the direction.

25
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
At mount time, the JS layer registers a callback on a native module.

26
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.000
When native needs to clear the form later, it simply triggers that callback.

27
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
By strictly matching the specific architectural task to the correct communication channel,

28
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
you guarantee predictable data flow across the bridge and keep your code base from devolving into spaghetti code.

29
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Communication channels handle discrete messages, but they do not solve the most dangerous problem in brownfield development,

30
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
keeping complex state synchronized over time without shared global memory.

31
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
In this diagram, watch what happens when we allow bidirectional rights.

32
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.000
If both native and JS components attempt to update the same piece of shared data simultaneously, they create a race condition.

33
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
The rights collide, and the state corrupts.

34
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The definitive solution is designating a strict single source of truth.

35
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:57.000
You implement a cross process safe key value store, like MMKV, and house the master database exclusively on the native side.

36
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:04.000
The native environment owns the data, and it feeds clean, read-only updates across the barrier to the JS side.

37
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Preventing data corruption requires deciding exactly which layer owns the truth before you write a single line of code.

38
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Managing state is closely tied to managing screens.

39
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Developers accustomed to pure react native often try to put a JS based navigation container at the root of a brownfield app.

40
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.000
In a true hybrid environment, this approach fails.

41
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Look at this visual hierarchy. This is the native first model, and it is the correct architectural approach.

42
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Here, a react native screen is treated exactly like any other fragment or view controller.

43
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.000
The central native controller pushes it onto the primary native stack, right alongside standard native UI items.

44
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.000
This ensures the native operating system retains absolute control.

45
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:56.000
When a user executes a system backswipe, or presses a hardware back button, the native controller smoothly discards the top react native layer.

46
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
The JS side does not intercept or interfere with the gesture.

47
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:10.000
To build an experience that feels like a single cohesive application to the user, the native layer must be the undisputed manager of all navigation transitions.

48
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Let's pull these concepts together with a practical capstone scenario, securely managing a user's login session across both the native and JS layers.

49
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.000
This structural flowchart outlines the initialization phase.

50
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.000
The native root system holds the master authentication token.

51
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:34.000
During the start-up sequence, it uses channel 1, initial props, to securely inject an exact duplicate of that token straight into the JS environment.

52
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Next comes the maintenance phase.

53
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:44.000
If the native token refreshes, the system uses channel 3, event emitters, to proactively push that update to the JS layer.

54
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000
The copy stays current, perfectly preserving the single source of truth.

55
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.000
This brings us to the three unyielding rules of brownfield architecture represented here.

56
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.000
One, strictly route your data through the five defined communication channels.

57
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Two, enforce a single source of truth for all shared state.

58
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.000
And three, always default to a native first navigation stack.

59
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Successfully engineering a hybrid application means you stop trying to force JavaScript to behave like native code.

60
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:18.000
You design precise, disciplined boundaries that allow both environments to do their jobs without stepping on each other.

